WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.193 --> 00:00:10.690
Today's speaker, we have Jan Koum.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.450
Jan is the founder of WhatsApp.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.070
Whatsapp as we mentioned in earlier

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.390
iteration of this is the startup
that did everything right.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.237
After not getting, after trying
to get jobs at Facebook and not,

00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:27.129
for either Jan or Brian,
they put their heads down.

00:00:27.129 --> 00:00:29.209
They figured out a product
that people really wanted.

00:00:29.209 --> 00:00:33.030
And while everybody else in
Silicon Valley was going to conferences,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.553
doing PR,
whatever else it is that people do,

00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:38.486
other than making a product
that people really love.

00:00:38.486 --> 00:00:41.274
WhatsApp just quietly built
this thing that was used by,

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.608
how many people at the time
of the acquisition?

00:00:43.608 --> 00:00:44.460
&gt;&gt; 450.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.555
&gt;&gt; 450 million people for a consumer app,
and more than a billion now, and

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:51.297
it was acquired by Facebook
a couple of years ago.

00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:53.050
And Jan can tell that story.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.210
But that you very much for
coming to talk to us.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:57.916
&gt;&gt; Sure, thanks for having me.

00:00:57.916 --> 00:01:02.335
So when Sam asked me to come and
speak here, I think I made some smart-ass

00:01:02.335 --> 00:01:07.116
comment how I'm going to speak about like
people actually building a product and

00:01:07.116 --> 00:01:09.734
not going to class or something like that.

00:01:09.734 --> 00:01:13.764
But as Sam mentioned,
we were just fortunate in a way

00:01:13.764 --> 00:01:18.450
that we stumbled into something
that people really wanted.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.879
And I'll explain in
a second how we got there.

00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:28.760
But the credit really
goes to not necessarily

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.948
us having some brilliant idea.

00:01:32.948 --> 00:01:36.687
I think the credit goes to also being
in the right time and the right place,

00:01:36.687 --> 00:01:38.930
and building a product that people wanted.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.850
And just realizing that people wanted it.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.501
So just to kind of give the quick
history of how we got where we got,

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:51.153
we actually, both me and
my co-founder Brian, we were Yahoo for

00:01:51.153 --> 00:01:56.350
about, I was there for 9 years,
he was there for 11 years.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.087
And that time was actually really,
really valuable for

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:07.132
us to learn how to scale back-end servers,
what makes a good product,

00:02:07.132 --> 00:02:12.371
what makes a bad product and
kind of seeing the Yahoo as a company

00:02:12.371 --> 00:02:17.454
get really successful and
then kind of, you know the story.

00:02:17.454 --> 00:02:20.596
So we left in 2007 just coincidentally

00:02:20.596 --> 00:02:25.057
we both left around the same
time within a month difference.

00:02:25.057 --> 00:02:28.951
And we took some time off,
we took about a year off.

00:02:28.951 --> 00:02:33.116
So we left at 2007, we took all 2008 off.

00:02:33.116 --> 00:02:37.554
He moved to New York with his girlfriend,
I was just goofing off and

00:02:37.554 --> 00:02:40.340
I really fell in love with my Nokia phone.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.028
I had this candy bar,
I think it was 6610 Nokia phone.

00:02:44.028 --> 00:02:47.868
That I jailbroke, I installed a net mon
software on it which showed you which

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:51.948
mobile cell you were connected to, and
all this advanced things you could do with

00:02:51.948 --> 00:02:55.080
a phone, that you couldn't do
when you took it out of the box.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.300
And so at the end of 2008 I went
traveling, I went to Argentina,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:06.543
I went to Russia, to Ukraine, to Hungary,
to Israel, to a bunch of countries.

00:03:06.543 --> 00:03:09.185
So I was gone for like two,
three months on and off and

00:03:09.185 --> 00:03:13.408
what I found really hard during that time
was just keeping in touch with my friends.

00:03:13.408 --> 00:03:16.875
Especially in Argentina, I don't know
if anybody here is from Argentina, or

00:03:16.875 --> 00:03:19.730
been to Argentina,
like there you go, so one person.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:21.112
What was your dialing code?

00:03:21.112 --> 00:03:24.835
It's so complicated,
I could never understand it.

00:03:24.835 --> 00:03:28.326
And so when we were in Argentina it's
just like having people call us or

00:03:28.326 --> 00:03:31.302
me calling people was like,
I went and got a local sim card.

00:03:31.302 --> 00:03:34.555
But I couldn't figure out how people
would call me, because of these weird

00:03:34.555 --> 00:03:37.767
complicated dialing codes and
prefixes, and I was just like, ugh!

00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:41.842
So this is how 2008 ends,
and January of 2009,

00:03:41.842 --> 00:03:44.963
my birthday was coming up in February, and

00:03:44.963 --> 00:03:50.623
I figure I'll get myself an early
birthday present, I'll go buy an iPhone.

00:03:50.623 --> 00:03:54.176
So I went to Apple Store,
bought an iPhone and

00:03:54.176 --> 00:03:57.354
right around the same time SDK came out,

00:03:57.354 --> 00:04:02.328
I think there was no SDK when
iPhone first came out in 2007.

00:04:02.328 --> 00:04:07.059
But I think they released it at September
of 2008 so I was like literally three,

00:04:07.059 --> 00:04:09.100
four months after iPhone came out.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:14.273
Sorry, after iOS SDK came out, I got
an iPhone and I started tinkering with it.

00:04:14.273 --> 00:04:16.110
I was also really bored and
I had a lot of free time.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.399
So I'm like okay, well, let's install
this weird thing called Xcode,

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:21.585
luckily I had a Mac.

00:04:21.585 --> 00:04:25.100
Let's install Xcode,
let's figure out what can be done.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:29.041
Let's build a simple app, and
then you kind of realize, holy ****.

00:04:29.041 --> 00:04:33.288
It has full Internet connectivity and
it can do full TCP/IP stack.

00:04:33.288 --> 00:04:38.260
So it basically is a little computer
that can talk to your server.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:42.098
So I'm like, okay, cool,
what can we do with it?

00:04:42.098 --> 00:04:43.918
So the first thing that we built,

00:04:43.918 --> 00:04:47.790
which I don't know how many of
you know the history of WhatsApp.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.854
The first thing that we built was
actually this concept of a status.

00:04:50.854 --> 00:04:55.038
And so the idea was if you ever used,
and you all might be too young for this.

00:04:55.038 --> 00:04:58.475
If you ever used something like AOL AIM or
ICQ or

00:04:58.475 --> 00:05:03.500
IRC or any of these products,
you have this concept of a status.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.670
Like Yahoo Messenger has this away from
keyboard or I'm busy or I'm in a meeting.

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:11.204
And so the first thing that you would do
when you used those messengers is you

00:05:11.204 --> 00:05:14.532
would like, the first message you
would send was, hey are you there?

00:05:14.532 --> 00:05:19.167
That was like the first message you sent
to start a conversation with somebody on

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:22.220
like ICQ or
if you private messaged somebody on IRC.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:27.137
Because people would be away and so they
would the status to kind of indicate like,

00:05:27.137 --> 00:05:30.940
I'm not near a computer or
I'm AFK or whatever.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:35.070
And so the idea was like, well,
let's take this concept of status and

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.759
just apply it to your phone.

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:39.695
So before people call you,
they can check your status.

00:05:39.695 --> 00:05:44.358
Maybe you're busy, maybe you're in a
meeting, maybe you're traveling, whatever.

00:05:44.358 --> 00:05:47.777
So if you don't pick up,
it lets people know why you don't pick up.

00:05:47.777 --> 00:05:55.518
And that was kind of like
the WhatsApp 1.0 application.

00:05:55.518 --> 00:06:00.860
And what we built was, the app that
hooked into your address book.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.499
So the other thing that kind of
worked in our favor was there were

00:06:03.499 --> 00:06:05.930
Address Book APIs on a mobile phone.

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:07.552
They didn't really exist on a desktop,
right.

00:06:07.552 --> 00:06:13.647
If you think about desktop like
Windows from 2000 to 2008-ish,

00:06:13.647 --> 00:06:18.500
people never really put address
books on their desktops.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:21.772
People always had address
books on their mobile phones.

00:06:21.772 --> 00:06:25.438
And so even if the API existed,
there was like nothing to query

00:06:25.438 --> 00:06:29.436
because the address book database
was empty on all these devices.

00:06:29.436 --> 00:06:34.394
And so luckily,
the one thing that Apple opened was

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:39.247
their address book APIs,
which was great for us.

00:06:39.247 --> 00:06:44.331
So what we did was, sorry, we basically
went through the address book and

00:06:44.331 --> 00:06:49.919
we could figure out if the contact in the
address book was another WhatsApp user.

00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:51.330
And that's basically
how the status worked.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:56.183
We would take your phone number, and
if it was an international phone number,

00:06:56.183 --> 00:06:59.137
we would try to normalize
it to the plus format.

00:06:59.137 --> 00:07:01.916
So your phone number would start,
all the phone numbers in our system,

00:07:01.916 --> 00:07:04.205
would start with a plus in
the full international format.

00:07:04.205 --> 00:07:09.021
And we will be able to do that if
the other person is a person on a WhatsApp

00:07:09.021 --> 00:07:11.320
network, a user of our product.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:16.080
And the idea was that,
before you start a phone call, or

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:22.010
before you send a message, you would check
the status of this person from WhatsApp.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:24.920
And they would say, I'm available and
you would call them.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:27.742
Right, so, that was WhatsApp 1.0,

00:07:27.742 --> 00:07:31.981
it failed horribly,
it was a disaster, it was depressing.

00:07:31.981 --> 00:07:35.309
Nobody used it, people downloaded it,
actually, surprisingly enough,

00:07:35.309 --> 00:07:38.434
and I think people downloaded it
because there were no apps at the time.

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:40.288
There were like maybe 1000 or

00:07:40.288 --> 00:07:43.538
2000 apps in the whole App Store
because it just went live.

00:07:43.538 --> 00:07:48.070
So we benefited from being early,
but the idea wasn't so great.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:51.586
So people would download and
they'd just like never use it.

00:07:51.586 --> 00:07:54.400
People just called people
like they normally would.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.770
It was really hard to
replace a native dialer, and

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:57.970
that's kind of what we wanted to do.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:02.189
We wanted to replace a native
dialing application.

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:05.230
So we We struggled for

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:09.910
a little bit, we kept adding all
these weird features in retrospect.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.390
Back then we thought they
were the best thing ever.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.410
You could set your status to automatically
change at a certain time of day.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.750
If you knew you were always in
a meeting from 2 to 4, you could

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:21.910
automatically configure it to always say
that you're in a meeting from 2 to 4.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.320
So it kind of had that functionality.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.600
And then something happened around
the summer of 2009, Apple introduced push

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.750
notifications so back then if you
wanted the application to wake up,

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:36.270
the only way to wake up was the user
tapping on the application item.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.800
And there was no way to do anything in the
background which in retrospect was really

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:44.750
back words because I do not know if any
of you know about Nokia Symbian phones.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.900
Well, 60 blackberry had over capability.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.830
Even before iPhone came
out to do auto start,

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:54.980
to do background multitasking,
to do networking, to do all of the stuff.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:59.440
And so IoS actually limited what
we can do, so we're struggling.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.988
And then Apple introduced Push
notifications were like, hallelujah.

00:09:02.988 --> 00:09:08.400
So we ended up,
who came into Apple push notifications?

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.300
I think this was like
around the summer time.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:14.810
Even before they were still in beta
because we have developer access.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.610
And we noticed that people were
using of this status as a way to

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.200
kind of communicate with each other.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.500
They would change the status to
say like I'm going to a bar.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.050
And like the change of status
would like broadcast and

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:30.880
go to all the other people who used
WhatsApp in their address book.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.590
And so around the summer of
2009 we're like, interesting.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.740
Maybe we should build messaging.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.590
And then it all kind of clicked
because I've always used SMS.

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:46.900
And if you remember on the old Nokia
phones, the SMS was not threaded.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.740
So you would get a message,
it would show up with a phone number.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.930
And if you get another
message from that person,

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:54.230
it would show up as just
another entry in your list.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:59.668
And then iOS came out and they introduced
threaded SMS, and everybody went wow.

00:09:59.668 --> 00:10:04.420
So we said okay, well, we can do
full networking, we can do TCP/IP.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:09.460
We can connect a phone, if your phone is
a client, to a server on the back end.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:14.030
We already had all this code to figure
out if you're a WhatsApp user or not.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:20.410
We could take your phone number parse it,
figure out all the international prefixes.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.600
We even figured out Argentina at some
point, which was not an easy thing to do,

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:24.540
but we got there.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:30.150
So, we got to a state where we
could actually send messaging,

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:31.940
have people send messages over WhatsApp.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:35.890
And, if you look at our application today,
you see all those features.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:36.880
You see group chat.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.000
You see ability to send media, to record
voice messages, to do all the stuff.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.750
We didn't have any of it.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:46.250
We didn't have like, the only thing
we had was one on one messaging.

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:48.140
That's it.
Just think how antiquated that is.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.806
And that's what we launched with.

00:10:50.806 --> 00:10:54.424
So, around September,
I think it was Septemberish,

00:10:54.424 --> 00:10:58.907
maybe late August early September is,
a maybe when early October,

00:10:58.907 --> 00:11:03.864
actually I don't remember is one of
the launch messaging and it took off and

00:11:03.864 --> 00:11:07.797
it was intuitive to us why it
took off because a semester is so

00:11:07.797 --> 00:11:12.943
expensive back on those days right and
especially international semester.

00:11:12.943 --> 00:11:15.829
So if you had two people who were
living in two different countries,

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:17.000
how did they communicate?

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.500
Well, they could send each other SMS,
which was expensive.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:24.310
They could use Skype, but
Skype mostly worked on a desktop, so

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:28.210
you had to like synchronize the time when
both of you are in front of a computer,

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.250
and, you know, people usually are not.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.620
Like, some of you have computers open,
but if you get a Skype call now,

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:34.480
you're not going to answer it, right.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.020
So, there was really no good way for
people to like communicate in real time.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.030
And so that's why SMS was so popular
because your phone was always with you,

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:44.130
it was always in your pocket,

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:47.290
it was always no matter where you
want your had your phone with you.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:51.200
So when you sent an SMS,
you never said, hey are you there?

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:51.850
But you never did that.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:52.610
You'd just send an SMS.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.190
The only thing sometimes you could say is,
hey, did you get my message.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:59.400
Because SMS was so unreliable,
especially internationally.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.310
And so that was the other
problem that we could solve by

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:05.550
building on top of TCP/IP is that we
could add reliability in our protocol.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:08.290
So, we rolled out messaging.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.800
We made it super reliable.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.870
We added all these visual indicators.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:15.830
We, if the message actually was
successfully delivered to our server.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.930
You knew if it was successfully delivered
to the device that you were sending it to,

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.960
and it just took off.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:26.250
And, when Sam talks about like,
well, we kind of like When has down.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:27.590
Or we didn't go to conferences and

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:31.170
when they didn't do a whole lot of other
stuff, it's just because we were lucky.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:34.900
We stumbled into something that
people really, really had a need for.

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:37.300
Just think about as a mess,
back in 2006, 2007.

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:38.460
Right?

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.780
There was no iMessage,
nothing else worked on your phone.

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:44.480
SMS was expensive.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:48.230
It was horrible limiting, you can
only send 160 characters at a time.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:51.010
And if you sent a longer message,
it would break it into multiple chunks.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:54.720
And sometimes the chunk at the bottom
would arrive first, so you'd have to read

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.910
the message at the bottom and
then read the message on top after that.

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:03.000
Media was horribly expensive and
it was not working well across platforms.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.720
So if I sent a video recorded on
Nokia phone to Blackberry users,

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.350
it probably couldn't have watched it.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:11.070
Or if you send a video recording
on Blackberry to iPhone probably

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.570
wouldn't work either.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:18.370
So we had all these limitations that meds
we're trying to address in our product and

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:20.070
obviously the biggest problem was cost,

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:25.120
meds was very expensive in Europe and in
many countries outside of North America.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.100
And we just kept going.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.460
So once we figured out
that there was a need for

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:31.330
it, we were like well, we better hire.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:35.810
So we hired some of our
old friends from Yahoo,

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:39.660
who left Yahoo,
some of our ex-Yahoo friends.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.100
I hired a friend of mine who I actually
met when I was working at Stanford

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:48.000
like when I was doing IT at graduate
school of business in like, 96 97-ish,

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:53.420
when I was in high school, so he came and
he helped work on the Blackberry client.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:56.650
So our goal was like,
we got to build all these features, but

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:58.240
we also gotta build all these platforms.

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:02.390
Because we started out with iPhone, but
the world back then was very different.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:08.010
Nokia actually was majority
smartphone platform back in the days.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:11.330
Not in North America, but if you kind
of looked at the rest of the world,

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:14.990
everybody was using Nokia smartphones and
Blackberry.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:16.449
And Android, I think didn't even exist.

00:14:16.449 --> 00:14:20.855
It was just barely existed in
the end of 2009 and early 2010.

00:14:20.855 --> 00:14:22.979
So we had to build for Nokia, and

00:14:22.979 --> 00:14:27.910
we had to find people who could
actually build for Nokia Symbian S-60.

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:29.809
And we had to find those people in Europe,

00:14:29.809 --> 00:14:32.322
because nobody in Silicon Valley
even heard of Nokia.

00:14:32.322 --> 00:14:36.934
So they went and found two really
good engineers to help us build that.

00:14:36.934 --> 00:14:41.764
Finally, a story about our Nokia client.

00:14:41.764 --> 00:14:43.160
It's actually built using Python.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:47.630
Not all our people know that actually
Nokia 60 has Python runtime.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:52.000
So remember when Brian, my cofounder,
started looking at Nokia.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.500
Because we knew that we had to do Nokia,
so he kind of went away for a week.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.030
He comes back a week later.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:59.350
He's like,
you're not going to believe this.

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:00.911
You can use Python to build a client.

00:15:00.911 --> 00:15:01.489
And I was like, no way.

00:15:01.489 --> 00:15:02.628
Get out of here.
He was like, no, no, no, no.

00:15:02.628 --> 00:15:05.924
Seriously.
You can run Python on this little tiny

00:15:05.924 --> 00:15:06.561
phone.

00:15:06.561 --> 00:15:09.800
I was like okay let's try and
do it and that's what we did.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:12.260
The basic was the whole back end,
the communications and

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:15.350
protocol was all in Python and
the UI was actually in C++.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:19.880
So we started working on
building multiple platforms and

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:21.990
we started working on building features.

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:25.470
So we launched Blackberry,
we launched Nokia 60.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.670
We launched Android all within
the two months of each other in 2010,

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:30.470
and then we started building features.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:34.310
So, we had to build what
people asked us to build.

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:35.300
People wanted group chat.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:36.630
Everybody's like, this is great.

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:38.870
I love your product.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:42.420
I want to have a group conversation
with my family, or three friends,

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.920
or five co-workers, or
ten people in a study group.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:49.860
And we're like, okay,
well how do we build group chat?

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:50.610
Let's figure it out.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:54.520
So we'll sit down and sketch the user
experience then figure out how to make

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:56.060
the back-end system work for group chat.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:16:01.110
And then people wanted, obviously
very quickly we added multimedia.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:04.320
And I think multimedia is what
really took us to the next level.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.770
Once we added ability to send a picture,
which.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:11.480
Today is like comical, how could you not
have the ability to send pictures through

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.670
a messaging product over your smartphone?

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:16.130
Back then you couldn't there was
nothing that worked really well.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:19.260
What was cheaper was reliable.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:20.940
So we added the ability
to send the picture,

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:22.830
we added the ability to send videos.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:27.910
We at some point introduced voice
messages and then things just took off.

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:30.480
And so at that point it was
just scaling the back end.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:33.380
And this is where it
kind of ties into what I

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:37.220
was talking about earlier where me and
Brian worked at Yahoo.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:40.610
Because we spent so much time there and
because we were there in early days and

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:45.900
we sold the company scale, we had all
this experience to scale the back end.

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:48.930
And we had our own share of outages.

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:53.480
And our service wasn't 100% perfect but
we would make sure that we would

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:57.040
learn from an outage and make sure that we
would add the right monitoring in place.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.110
And we would have capacity always for
holidays like Christmas and

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:02.380
New Years where there's a traffic spike.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:05.480
But having that experience
working at Yahoo,

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:08.270
learning how to scale
the back-end systems.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.550
Learning how to tweak
the operating systems, the kernels,

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:11.860
the networking stack.

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:15.460
The ethernet driver if you have to,
it's all kind of tied together.

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:21.690
So our experience at Yahoo,
our experience with difficult or

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:27.210
challenging SMS protocol that we all use
as a consumer kind of all combined with

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:31.720
this perfect timing that happened in 2009,
2010 with smartphones coming online and

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.500
people wanting to have this
ability to communicate.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:36.220
And if you think about smartphone,

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:39.420
the messaging is a killer app for
the smartphone.

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:44.210
So we just basically stumbled
into this killer app.

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:47.560
Because nothing else you do more
with a smartphone that communicate.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:51.070
Most of it is probably talking to your
friends and family and your loved ones.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:56.370
I message on WhatsApp or
Skype or anything else.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:57.800
Yes?
They guy [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:59.460
&gt;&gt; Would you do it again?

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.500
&gt;&gt; Could you make another
app that takes off?

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.030
&gt;&gt; A messaging app or just an app?

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:03.650
&gt;&gt; Just any app?

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:05.920
&gt;&gt; I could.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:07.264
I'm not sure you can.

00:18:07.264 --> 00:18:10.700
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I already did this once.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:14.350
I mean the chances of me being
successful again are like zero.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.930
So the odds are on your side.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.440
So you should go and do something.

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:22.910
So that's why we're able to
actually spend all of our time

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:29.070
heads down building a product because
we had this amazing product market fit.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:32.100
We had this amazing product
that people wanted.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:35.710
They were like, give me, give me, give me,
when we were all in and out Nokia S40

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:40.200
which was just like a step below Nokia
S60 Which was kind of a picture phone.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:45.370
People were emailing us, asking for,
when is it going to be done,

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:47.580
when is it going to be done,
when is it going to be done?

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.430
And so,
there was a huge pent up demand for

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:52.500
any platform before we would launch it.

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:56.400
And so that why we didn't have
a need to go to the conference and

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:58.969
do a lot of PR or do anything like that.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.450
Because we had people who needed
our product and we had like

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.780
millions of people who were waiting for us
to build a new feature or a new platform.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:11.380
And that's why we were able to just
go heads down and build a product.

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.920
So that was kind of like some background
that I wanted to give you on how we

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:15.940
got started.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:20.120
We can do a q and a for
the next 15 to 20 minutes and yeah.

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:26.270
&gt;&gt; The specific thing I want to hear most
about is how you dealt with the launch of

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:30.880
iMessage and Facebook Messenger and
all the other messaging platforms.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:34.410
WhatsApp was clearly first, and
then the world got scary quickly.

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:38.325
How did you think about that?

00:19:38.325 --> 00:19:42.460
[UNKNOWN
&gt;&gt; So, how do we think about imessage and

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:43.540
all these other platforms.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:49.330
So, with iMessage we,
when did they launch?

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.480
I think 2011 ish.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:52.650
Something like that.

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:56.420
I think 2011 at the developer conference.

00:19:56.420 --> 00:20:01.990
So, the world in Silicon Valley is
very different from the world outside.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.640
So in Silicon Valley if you look around,
90% of people have an iPhone.

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:10.340
Not only do they have an iPhone,
they have the latest, greatest iPhone.

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:16.910
Outside of silicon valley,
it's like 80 or 90% Android, right?

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:22.085
So for us, having an iMessage launch was
just like a small blip on the radar.

00:20:22.085 --> 00:20:26.540
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; So Facebook Messenger so

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:32.320
I think Facebook for a very long time
didn't really have a good messaging story.

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:37.220
I remember they bought this company called
Beluga which I think it was Beluga that

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:40.050
was group messaging so they were kind
of focused on group messaging at first.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:44.190
And then they shut it down and
turn it into Facebook Messenger.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:46.560
But Facebook Messenger was
part of the Facebook app.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.810
It wasn't really a dedicated
app back in the days.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:54.180
But ultimately, if you look at
a Facebook Messenger, the graph that

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:58.980
the Facebook Messenger is using is very
different from the graph on your phone.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:04.590
And if you think about people you add to
your address book and people on Facebook,

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:09.310
there is going to be some overlap but
the most part is going to be different.

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:12.230
So people who I add on Facebook are
probably not people I'm going to message

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:13.300
with a lot.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:17.920
And people who I put in my address book,
people who are probably

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:22.340
a different graph in terms of like
how they are important to me and

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:27.360
if I urge you into my, basically if
I exchange my number with somebody,

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:31.460
it means that you give them the ability
to WhatsApp me, SMS me or call me.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:36.270
There are probably of people who are I'm
friend with in Facebook who called me,

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:43.580
I will probably like, who is this, yeah
we met each other once and I added them.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:47.390
I'm probably not necessarily
a typical example.

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:51.890
I mean, I'm sure there are people who have
different grabs on different networks.

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.380
I'm sure there are people who
use WhatsApp only for work and

00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:58.060
they only have their coworkers on
the WhatsApp, in their address book.

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:02.800
And i'm sure that there
are people who only

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:06.100
have certain set of their
contacts in one or the other.

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:10.100
But I think overall this idea of like
well if I add you to my phone I give you

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:14.750
permission to interrupt my life is what
makes our network a little different.

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:18.390
Because people have these connections

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:22.400
that are stronger with people who they
have in each other's address book.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:24.680
With Facebook,
I have people I went to high school with,

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:26.590
I have people I went to university with.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:29.100
And it's great that I can keep in
touch with them on Facebook but

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:32.240
I wouldn't want them to call me out
of the blue at 7:00 pm it would be

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:35.510
awkward because I haven't
talked to them for years.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:40.690
So the graphs are different
in that sense so

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:43.800
we always had kind of going back and
generalizing it.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:47.270
We'll always have competition
from day one, there was actually

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:51.850
a point in time where there was a new
messaging app popping up like every month.

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:54.860
And every month there was
an article on Tech Crunch how this

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:58.930
awesome new messaging app was going to
take down all other messaging apps.

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:01.095
And I don't know if they paid
Tech Crunch to write it or what.

00:23:01.095 --> 00:23:04.990
[LAUGH] And we would just read this and
we would go like, they have no users.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:06.870
How can you write that
story in Tech Crunch.

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:08.470
It just makes no sense.

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:10.680
And obviously we didn't want to say
anything because we didn't want

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:11.800
to draw attention to ourselves.

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:16.060
But actually on purpose tried
to stay under the radar.

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:20.740
It was trying to see this from
the sidelines, all this kind of like,

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:23.570
dog and pony show that
happened with all these apps.

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:28.040
I mean there was ping me, there was
message me, there was group me, there was

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:32.930
skigs, there were like, hello, there were
like ten different messaging apps at some

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:36.730
point, which kept getting all this
publicity and we were like good for you.

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:39.300
Have all the publicity you want,
we'll just stay under the radar and

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:42.370
not have any attention drawn to us.

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:46.230
So we'll always have competition be it big
guys like Imessage of Facebook messenger,

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:47.690
be it little guys like kick.

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:49.130
We always have competition.

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:55.010
Even today we still have apps like
Telegram out there, and Line, and Cow.

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:58.420
But we said that our destiny
is really in our hands.

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:01.140
We can't worry too much about competition.

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.750
We have to worry about our product and
our users.

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:06.330
And if we spend a lot of time
thinking about competition and

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.890
looking at competition,
we're going to fail, yeah?

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:13.515
So trying to build on what you just
talked about, this social graph.

00:24:13.515 --> 00:24:18.304
I mean, I feel like this whole
social graph is actually now

00:24:18.304 --> 00:24:23.000
changing like I mean,
at least among me and my friends.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:25.730
I think I don't call people so much.

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:28.370
I don't really send them SMS.

00:24:28.370 --> 00:24:32.680
I will probably at people mostly on this
social media, like online, Facebook.

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:35.480
I could add that or like chat apps
like it's what said it more, but

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:37.950
if you feel like this
whole social graph and

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:43.440
this social changing slowly because people
call less and people send SMS less.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:47.190
People like to do it more on
this social media platforms.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:48.838
Do you have a view on that and

00:24:48.838 --> 00:24:52.010
do you think there's any
like opportunity on that?

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:56.759
Or do you think this
shift is even true or?

00:24:56.759 --> 00:25:01.274
&gt;&gt; I think, yeah, I think true,
so the question was,

00:25:01.274 --> 00:25:05.996
how, so the world is changing,
people call less, and

00:25:05.996 --> 00:25:12.070
now like people add the social networks
all kind of merge into one now.

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:15.566
Yeah, you're right,
I think people call less these days, and

00:25:15.566 --> 00:25:16.700
people mostly message each other.

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:24.610
I don't really know if we would
do anything differently today,

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:26.760
or even back then.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:31.910
I think for us, the focus has always been
on well, we want to provide a utility,

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:36.130
we want to provide an application that
is purely, only about communication.

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:40.052
So if you look at some other
apps like V-Chat or Line or

00:25:40.052 --> 00:25:42.790
Kakao because they do a lot
of different things, right?

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:45.967
You can like order taxes through V-Chat
and you can follow people online,

00:25:45.967 --> 00:25:47.930
like Line has a whole feed concept.

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:51.510
And we always wanted to build something
that is really, really efficient and

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:55.480
utilitarian and also fast and reliable.

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:58.790
I mean, not a lot of people can have the
latest and greatest smart phone, right?

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:01.340
A lot of people have android
phones that are low end.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:04.019
A lot of people have, or
used to have Blackberry phones

00:26:04.019 --> 00:26:07.804
that didn't have a lot of horse power and
a lot of memory and a lot of CPU, so for

00:26:07.804 --> 00:26:11.649
us it was always about reliability and
efficiency of the app and not trying to do

00:26:11.649 --> 00:26:15.578
all these different things that a lot of
different social networks and apps do.

00:26:15.578 --> 00:26:20.420
Yeah?

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:25.070
&gt;&gt; How do you think about Messenger right
now, because now that Messenger integrated

00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:29.350
a lot of the features that's WhatsApp had,
I am starting to use Messenger a lot more.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:32.480
And that definitely takes away
from the internal WhatsApp, so

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:34.259
do you have internal competition?

00:26:34.259 --> 00:26:40.060
&gt;&gt; No, I think internal there is still
a lot of room for both apps to grow.

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:42.920
I think messenger is really strong
in countries like North America,

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:44.460
like the United States for example.

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:47.463
So I think they compliment
each other geographically.

00:26:47.463 --> 00:26:51.952
So I think if you look at countries
like India, or Israel, or

00:26:51.952 --> 00:26:55.160
Hong Kong, or Germany, or Spain.

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:58.110
WhatsApp has really strong
foothold in those countries and

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:00.460
I think if you look at
something like Australia or

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:02.700
North America you're probably going to
see Messenger do really well.

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:06.510
So a lot of it is also not
necessarily split by a graph, but

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:08.035
also by the country you're in.

00:27:08.035 --> 00:27:10.020
Okay.

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:14.052
&gt;&gt; I appreciate the humility that you
couldn't do it again with another app,

00:27:14.052 --> 00:27:17.660
but I'm sure people asked you
all the time for help or advice.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:20.090
How do you determine if you're on idea or

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.380
look at an application
it can do really well?

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:25.030
How do you think through that?

00:27:26.050 --> 00:27:26.857
&gt;&gt; It's very simple.

00:27:26.857 --> 00:27:32.676
The question is how do you determine if
an app has a potential or is a good idea?

00:27:37.017 --> 00:27:41.030
It has to solve a really basic problem and
it has to do it in a really simple and

00:27:41.030 --> 00:27:42.690
efficient way.

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:48.590
I mean going back to what we built,
we in some ways solved a problem, right?

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:53.000
People had a problem communicating when
they were not in the same room, when they

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:56.490
were in different cities, or in different
countries, or in different time zones.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:28:01.420
And so,
it's not that it wasn't impossible.

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:02.850
It was just, it was hard, and

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:06.380
it was expensive, and
we made it easier and cheaper.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:11.130
And when you offer something
to people that is easier and

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:14.940
the cheaper people of course, will use it.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:19.052
So I think it's the number one thing to
look at for me when I look at a product,

00:28:19.052 --> 00:28:22.800
does it solve a need and does it solve
a need on a global scale, right?

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:25.990
If you solve a need for
people on Stanford campus,

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.890
that's great, but can it scale to
a billion or 2 billion people.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:32.595
So we need it for the only people only in
Silicon Valley by providing them charges

00:28:32.595 --> 00:28:36.210
for their Teslas, great, how many
people have Teslas in the world, right?

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:40.499
It's gotta be a global, actually a lot
of people have Teslas, I don't know.

00:28:40.499 --> 00:28:43.462
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But it's gotta be a global solution

00:28:43.462 --> 00:28:47.660
that applies to everybody in
every country potentially, right?

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:49.393
And so, that was kind of like.

00:28:49.393 --> 00:28:53.019
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Sorry to get
wrong as instead Silicon Valley,

00:28:53.019 --> 00:28:57.510
everyone's got the iPhone 7S or
whatever we're on now.

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:02.066
How did you build that into the culture of
the company to think about your users all

00:29:02.066 --> 00:29:03.145
around the world?

00:29:06.908 --> 00:29:08.340
I don't know.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.650
We got lucky with the people we hired.

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:14.710
And that was the other thing that I didn't
mention that I should've mentioned.

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:19.810
We ended up with a really
incredible team that we

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:24.490
mostly hired out of our personal network
from ex-Yahoo and from friends of friends.

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:29.156
I think ourselves had
a really good understanding.

00:29:29.156 --> 00:29:32.630
I mean me and myself being an immigrant
and growing up in other country and

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:34.930
going to all these other countries,

00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:40.450
I understood that there was more
to smartphone than just iPhone.

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:45.274
That was the thing everybody talked
about and wrote about in 2008 and 2009.

00:29:45.274 --> 00:29:49.980
It's like for me, especially as
somebody who really liked Nokia phones,

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:53.160
before Nokia went out of business,
I was like, We've got to build for Nokia,

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:58.949
because they're great phones, and there
is like a billion smartphones out there.

00:29:58.949 --> 00:30:04.090
So I think, just me and my co-founder,
having that perspective,

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:06.470
probably just goes through the company,
and people understood that.

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:10.520
Hey, you don't want to just build for
iPhones.

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:12.290
You don't want to just build for
the latest and greatest.

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:17.680
You have all these millions of phones
out there, billions of phones out there,

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:20.850
that you've gotta build for because you
want those users to be using your product.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:22.320
And they also are asking us to do that.

00:30:24.198 --> 00:30:26.656
Yeah?
&gt;&gt; Can you talk about the business side as

00:30:26.656 --> 00:30:30.652
far as incorporation,
equity and raising money?

00:30:30.652 --> 00:30:32.828
A business side my favorite topic, sure.

00:30:37.605 --> 00:30:44.140
So we incorporated on my birthday
on February 24th of 2009.

00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:48.270
And the reason like the thinking that
was going through, so let me back up.

00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:53.650
We were trying to submit an app
into the Apple Store and

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:55.690
I didn't want to do it under my own name.

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:59.240
I didn't want the app to say
you Coon because I figured like

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:01.320
who would want to submit
an app made by some guy.

00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:03.780
So I figured we should
probably be more official,

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:07.310
we should have a company,
okay, so go to Google.

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:12.350
How do you start a company,
right, it's just step one.

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:18.310
So I got a friend of mine,
he was an insurance broker,

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:20.700
so he had his own company and

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:25.710
he was like 3 blocks away from
me in Santa Clara where I live.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:30.240
And so, I went to his office because I
used to buy insurance for my car and

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:35.010
my house from him and so, I was like dude
how did you incorporate he is like it easy

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:38.510
you take these articles on corporation
it's like one page was like five things

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:44.310
written and you go to San Francisco
to the State Building, the secretary

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:47.790
of the state or whatever and you give
them $100 and they stamp and you're done.

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:51.690
I'm like no way it can't be that easy,
he said yeah, it's that easy,

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:55.657
I'm like all right, so we had to submit
an app and they wanted us to show.

00:31:55.657 --> 00:31:58.942
Like Apple Store actually
wanted us to send them a copy

00:31:58.942 --> 00:32:00.987
of the incorporation articles.

00:32:00.987 --> 00:32:04.180
So it was like, okay, easy.

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:07.210
I have nothing to do that day,
I'll drive to San Francisco, get lunch,

00:32:07.210 --> 00:32:09.930
go to his office, get a stamp,
Get a letter great.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:12.180
Send it to Apple.

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:14.280
They look at the letters,
like yup, you're legit,

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:16.540
you're a company, you can now
submit under the name WhatsApp.

00:32:16.540 --> 00:32:17.720
I'm like cool.

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:19.620
Submitted an app under the name WhatsApp.

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:21.156
So that was how we incorporated.

00:32:21.156 --> 00:32:26.080
In terms of, in terms of,
it's easier than it sounds,

00:32:26.080 --> 00:32:29.740
like a struggle for me,
because I've never done it.

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:33.960
How did we think about money or
the whole funding thing.

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:40.120
So we left Yahoo with some savings,

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:43.910
because Yahoo did really well in late 90s,
early 2000s.

00:32:43.910 --> 00:32:48.110
And so we had stock,
we had options, we had our issues.

00:32:48.110 --> 00:32:52.130
And so I was actually able
to not only take a year off,

00:32:52.130 --> 00:32:54.650
obviously not do anything extravagant.

00:32:54.650 --> 00:32:57.480
But like live off my savings for a year.

00:32:57.480 --> 00:32:59.970
And then I had enough money to where

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:04.640
while I was still tinkering around with
an app and we didn't have exuberant costs.

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:08.040
I actually remember like when we started
out I was using my buddy's server,

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:11.840
this guy Chuck,
who also used to work at Yahoo.

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.080
He used to run Yahoo Sports.

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:17.300
So I used to sit next to the Yahoo
sports teams, so we became friends.

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:19.330
So I was like hey chuck
can I use your server?

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:21.577
I don't want to pay 20$ a month for
a server, he's like yeah sure.

00:33:21.577 --> 00:33:26.180
So like you know, saving $20 a month
on a server was a big deal for me.

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:28.650
So we would run original on his server.

00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:31.260
And I remember at some point
launched messaging and

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.930
I saw all this growth and he's like
dude you got to get your own servers.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.340
I'm like no I don't want to pay for
my own servers.

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:37.800
No you got to get your own servers.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:40.440
You're taking up all this CPU and
bandwidth.

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:42.460
I'm like no no no I'm fine.

00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:44.720
So eventually he kicked
me out of his server.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:50.241
And which was great because we
were switching from Linux to

00:33:50.241 --> 00:33:54.360
which would allow them to
have the experience at Yahoo.

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:59.370
And so what we were able to actually,
for a long time live off of our savings.

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:02.140
And since we had all of this

00:34:02.140 --> 00:34:05.020
experience on run the company
efficiently when it comes to servers and

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:08.450
backend and bandwidth and everything,
there was not a lot of expense.

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:12.150
The expense started when
we had to go hire people,

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:17.120
and the idea was that okay,
we going to have to pay for

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:19.750
bandwidths, we going to have to pay for
Banbros, we going to have to pay for

00:34:19.750 --> 00:34:24.070
SMS verification because to sign up on
WhatsApp you have to verify your number.

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:29.320
And, we did a small angel round,

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:34.850
I can't even remember,
I think it was end of 2009.

00:34:34.850 --> 00:34:36.470
We did a small angel round, and

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:41.890
then we basically kind of kept our company
running without losing too much money,

00:34:41.890 --> 00:34:45.880
because in the early days,
iPhone app was actually paid.

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:48.470
The people had to pay one
dollar to download iPhone app.

00:34:48.470 --> 00:34:51.940
While everything else was free like
Android and Blackberry and Nokia.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:56.330
And so we had people paying for
iPhone app and that kind of went to,

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:02.210
it gave us the ability to pay for the
bill, for electricity for bandwidth bills,

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:08.250
for several bills and all that stuff.

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:10.190
And I think probably around 2010, 2011.

00:35:10.190 --> 00:35:12.790
People started knocking on our door.

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:15.820
We didn't even went out to look for money.

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:17.520
Which is a great situation to be in.

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:20.250
because if you are going to go raise,
and you need money,

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:23.975
you probably are not going
to get the terms you want.

00:35:23.975 --> 00:35:28.880
Which was, for us was,
kind of worked in our favor because

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:32.630
all these receipts started
coming to us and they're like.

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:33.590
Because they're doing great

00:35:33.590 --> 00:35:35.240
we want to partner with you
we want to give you money.

00:35:35.240 --> 00:35:37.660
And we're like eh we don't really need it.

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:42.260
Which makes them want
to invest even more so

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:46.030
we kind of did this dance where we're like
no no we don't really want your money.

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:50.230
Come back in a few months and eventually
after all these conversations me and

00:35:50.230 --> 00:35:52.180
Brian sit down and we're like okay well.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:53.590
We just wasted their time and

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:56.640
our time, should we just take funding or
should we not.

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:59.720
And we decided we should because it's

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:03.440
better to have money in your bank
account for your business or not.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:08.000
And that's the words of wisdom that I
got from Jeff Who because Jeff was like

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:10.570
if you can have money in your bank account
you should have money in your bank account

00:36:10.570 --> 00:36:12.630
because you never know if you have to buy.

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:13.160
In building or

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:16.690
if your netted to buy some office space
because you started growing too quickly.

00:36:16.690 --> 00:36:19.590
And you don't want to negotiate and
raise money when it's too late.

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:22.370
Like, you want to do it
when you don't need money.

00:36:22.370 --> 00:36:25.210
So listening to words of
wisdom of Jeff Ralston,

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:29.210
we were like, okay, let's get some
funding and we partnered with Seguin and

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:34.590
we got money from
Sequoia Any other questions?

00:36:34.590 --> 00:36:37.210
Yeah?
&gt;&gt; Could you sort of walk us through your

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:41.760
internal psychology and confidence
over the trajectory of the company?

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:46.640
At the beginning, did you identify that
the market timing was pretty spot on, or

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:50.710
were you just sort of following
what your users were saying to you?

00:36:54.113 --> 00:37:00.577
&gt;&gt; I think until we did messaging,
when we doing that status feature,

00:37:00.577 --> 00:37:07.250
obviously it was rough because we had
no users, and nobody using our product.

00:37:07.250 --> 00:37:12.840
And so you're sitting there In
your room building a product and

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:16.130
thinking like well, nobody wants to buy.

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:16.920
Why am I doing this?

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:18.800
What is the meaning of life and
all that stuff, right?

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:21.760
And so you kind of like it's stuff.

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:24.990
When you're building a product that people
don't really want, you feel rejected.

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:26.680
You feel like, why aren't you using it?

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:28.480
It's great, I put all my energy in it.

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:37.270
So once we added messaging,
it was like 180 degrees difference.

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:40.310
All of a sudden everybody
wants our product.

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:41.900
Everybody thinks it's
the coolest thing ever.

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:46.540
We get all these letters into
our own email, in-boxes.

00:37:46.540 --> 00:37:49.310
All these emails from people
saying how great your product is.

00:37:49.310 --> 00:37:51.230
I'm able to keep in touch with my fiance.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:53.300
I got married because of your product.

00:37:53.300 --> 00:37:57.030
Your product helped me save lives
because the hikers were lost and

00:37:57.030 --> 00:37:59.610
the hikers were able to use
WhatsApp share location to send.

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:01.570
It's just night and day.

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:03.750
When people want your product, and

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:07.580
they love your product, the psychology
inside a company is just different.

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:11.290
People would come to work, and they'd be
like we're building the best thing ever.

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:12.280
People loves the product.

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:13.360
This is great.

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:16.770
And so, we didn't really have to do
a lot of selling even to the candidates.

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:22.470
Like, people who came in to interview
with us basically fell into two counts.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:25.789
People who live sole bubble and never
heard of WhatsApp and they'll be like,

00:38:25.789 --> 00:38:27.560
where would I work for WhatsApp?

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:30.330
And people who kind of fell into like,
well, there is a whole world out there

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:33.910
bubble, and they were like, you guys have
millions of users, like my cousin in Spain

00:38:33.910 --> 00:38:37.550
or my friend in Germany was telling me
about your product and everybody uses it.

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:40.000
Or people would say, I went to India or
I went to Middle East and

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:40.990
everybody uses your product.

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:42.990
It's like amazing how come
nobody heard about you?

00:38:42.990 --> 00:38:44.590
And they're like, well, that's on purpose.

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:48.150
And so basically like these two types
of people would with intro and so

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:51.860
obviously people who were had it
like the silicone valley bubble.

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:53.420
They don't even want to come work for us.

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:56.130
Which is not the end of the world because
there were plenty of people who understood

00:38:56.130 --> 00:38:58.250
there was a whole big world out there.

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:00.630
And they were happy to build product for

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:02.910
hundreds of millions of
users all over the world.

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:06.990
Yeah.
[INAUDIBLE]

00:39:06.990 --> 00:39:11.046
&gt;&gt; How was the fundraising experience

00:39:11.046 --> 00:39:12.166
[INAUDIBLE].

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:18.184
Why did I partner with Sequoia?

00:39:20.262 --> 00:39:22.920
So we had a few companies
give us term sheets.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:25.980
One of them even gave
us a blank term sheet.

00:39:25.980 --> 00:39:27.225
They're like,
fill out the number you want.

00:39:27.225 --> 00:39:30.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And we're like, well, if they're that,

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:36.160
that irresponsible with
other people's money,

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:40.300
we shouldn't do that,
shouldn't be partnering with that.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:43.470
Sequoia is just an amazing brand, right?

00:39:43.470 --> 00:39:49.170
For me,
living in Silicon Valley since 1992 and

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:54.781
reading articles, and seeing news about
all these companies that have gone public,

00:39:54.781 --> 00:39:59.270
like Netscape, and Sysco, and Google, and

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:04.660
knowing that a lot of them were backed
by Sequoia just made it not a very

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:11.643
difficult decision to pick Sequoia Well,
I also really like people who work there.

00:40:11.643 --> 00:40:14.023
A lot of it was just personal chemistry.

00:40:14.023 --> 00:40:21.675
A lot of it is the C company understanding
how much to be hands on or not.

00:40:21.675 --> 00:40:25.362
Like Sequoia was actually really
great about, they knew the numbers,

00:40:25.362 --> 00:40:26.780
they knew we were growing.

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:28.910
They didn't meddle, right?

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:31.620
They didn't need to come in and
say you guys are doing this wrong or

00:40:31.620 --> 00:40:34.380
doing that wrong,
there was no need for that.

00:40:34.380 --> 00:40:38.320
And I think we had that understanding
upfront, where they kind of made us

00:40:38.320 --> 00:40:43.070
a promise they were like,
we're here to help you financially.

00:40:43.070 --> 00:40:45.320
We're not here to help
you with management.

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:46.423
We're not here to help you write code.

00:40:46.423 --> 00:40:48.409
We're not here to help you build features.

00:40:48.409 --> 00:40:51.490
We're here just to help you grow and
to help you financially.

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:54.743
And if you need any help outside of that,
come knock on our door and

00:40:54.743 --> 00:40:55.759
we'll try to help.

00:40:55.759 --> 00:40:59.232
And they were really helpful with stuff
when we asked, like recruiting or

00:40:59.232 --> 00:41:00.250
anything like that.

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:02.560
They would sometimes meet with
prospective candidates and

00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:04.780
tells them why they should join WhatsApp.

00:41:04.780 --> 00:41:08.330
So Sequoia was really great,
it was a brand.

00:41:08.330 --> 00:41:11.940
And I remember, actually,
when we have multiple term sheets.

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:14.628
Me and
Brian went to Jeff Roston's house and

00:41:14.628 --> 00:41:17.768
we were talking to him,
it was late in the evening.

00:41:17.768 --> 00:41:19.819
We were trying to just get
advice on what we should do and

00:41:19.819 --> 00:41:20.944
he kind of looked at all them.

00:41:20.944 --> 00:41:22.662
And we talked through
all the different terms.

00:41:22.662 --> 00:41:24.855
And then he kind of said,
once you're a Sequoia company,

00:41:24.855 --> 00:41:25.961
you 're a Sequoia company.

00:41:25.961 --> 00:41:30.964
It's that branding is really strong and
it means a lot.

00:41:30.964 --> 00:41:32.555
&gt;&gt; I have an important one.

00:41:32.555 --> 00:41:33.519
&gt;&gt; Yes, what?

00:41:33.519 --> 00:41:34.517
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:41:34.517 --> 00:41:38.229
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] for you, but-
&gt;&gt; How did you get your first few thousand

00:41:38.229 --> 00:41:40.755
users back in the day and set the status?

00:41:40.755 --> 00:41:43.071
&gt;&gt; Well, how did we get our
first few thousand users?

00:41:43.071 --> 00:41:46.008
So, there was no apps back then.

00:41:46.008 --> 00:41:53.020
And iOS, well it wasn't iOS back then,
it was iPhone OS.

00:41:53.020 --> 00:41:56.683
And the Apple Store had
this category What's New.

00:41:56.683 --> 00:42:01.003
And the trick was to submit
a new app every few days, so

00:42:01.003 --> 00:42:05.550
you would always show up
on top of What's New.

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:07.480
And you would make a small
change to the name.

00:42:07.480 --> 00:42:09.860
Because I think back in the old days,

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:13.600
the name difference
triggered you as a new app.

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:18.382
So it would basically have status and then
it would say status for your smartphone,

00:42:18.382 --> 00:42:22.636
or status for your calls, or status for
your iPhone, or updated status.

00:42:22.636 --> 00:42:27.354
We would basically tweak the name a little
bit with every new version we submit.

00:42:27.354 --> 00:42:31.016
Which always kept us almost always
at the top of the New category.

00:42:31.016 --> 00:42:35.338
And since there are no apps, people would
go to What's New category all the time

00:42:35.338 --> 00:42:38.771
to download, to try to download
whatever people would build.

00:42:38.771 --> 00:42:43.498
Because today you have thousands of apps,
back days you had hundreds.

00:42:43.498 --> 00:42:47.600
And basically by gaming the system
a little bit, we were able to.

00:42:47.600 --> 00:42:50.617
I think that loophole got
closed really quickly, but

00:42:50.617 --> 00:42:53.309
luckily by that time we
already had messaging.

00:42:53.309 --> 00:42:53.890
Yeah?

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:56.821
&gt;&gt; How did you scale up your company,
I mean,

00:42:56.821 --> 00:43:01.030
how do you scale up WhatsApp
into different countries?

00:43:01.030 --> 00:43:03.224
&gt;&gt; How do we scale into
different countries?

00:43:03.224 --> 00:43:05.330
We didn't have to scale.

00:43:05.330 --> 00:43:07.180
Well, we did have to do a couple
of things, I take that back.

00:43:08.440 --> 00:43:11.760
So, there were two things
that we had to do.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:13.560
One, we had to build different platforms.

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:15.924
Because there were some countries
where iPhones just didn't exist.

00:43:15.924 --> 00:43:20.769
And everybody was using either Nokia or
Blackberry or a combination of two.

00:43:20.769 --> 00:43:23.919
The second thing that we started
doing early on is focusing on

00:43:23.919 --> 00:43:25.229
localization, right?

00:43:25.229 --> 00:43:28.941
So again, this kind of goes back to
Silicon Valley bubble where everybody in

00:43:28.941 --> 00:43:30.568
Silicon Valley speaks English.

00:43:30.568 --> 00:43:33.937
Therefore, the rest of the world
must speak English, not quite.

00:43:33.937 --> 00:43:37.370
So, we focused early on localization.

00:43:37.370 --> 00:43:42.690
We actually hired people internally into
the company who were doing two things.

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:44.753
They were customer support
representatives, so

00:43:44.753 --> 00:43:47.857
they would help people with problems and
write FAQs and help debug issues.

00:43:47.857 --> 00:43:50.400
But they were also all multilingual.

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:52.340
So we'd hire somebody who
was perfect in Spanish, and

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:53.940
we would hire somebody who
was perfect in German.

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:56.145
And we'd hire somebody who is
perfect in Portuguese, and

00:43:56.145 --> 00:43:58.082
we would hire somebody who
was perfect in Italian.

00:43:58.082 --> 00:44:02.505
We would hire somebody who was perfect in
all these languages where our apps were

00:44:02.505 --> 00:44:03.570
starting to grow.

00:44:03.570 --> 00:44:06.220
So we could build a really good,
localized experience.

00:44:06.220 --> 00:44:11.000
So when you download WhatsApp in Brazil,
it's not in English, it's in Portuguese.

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:14.729
And I think that is what helped
us grow in all these countries.

00:44:14.729 --> 00:44:16.155
Yeah.

00:44:16.155 --> 00:44:20.223
&gt;&gt; How did you convince your first few
employees I know that they came from your

00:44:20.223 --> 00:44:21.331
personal network.

00:44:21.331 --> 00:44:23.991
But how did you convince them to join you?

00:44:23.991 --> 00:44:27.625
&gt;&gt; It wasn't hard,
most of them were unemployed.

00:44:27.625 --> 00:44:32.169
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So let me see, so

00:44:32.169 --> 00:44:36.810
Brian joined, so Brian left Yahoo.

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:40.790
And he, I think, didn't really do
anything, I think he left in 98.

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:43.115
So he didn't really do anything for
ten years.

00:44:43.115 --> 00:44:44.695
He was one of the early app engineers.

00:44:44.695 --> 00:44:47.029
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that was one.

00:44:47.029 --> 00:44:49.154
Chris, my friend, who went up to Stanford.

00:44:49.154 --> 00:44:53.134
I think he was doing this startup
that wasn't really going anywhere,.

00:44:53.134 --> 00:44:54.399
And he was in LA, and

00:44:54.399 --> 00:44:59.720
he got married to this wonderful girl
who's parents are actually from here.

00:44:59.720 --> 00:45:03.390
So, I think for her it was an advantage
to move here to be closer to her parents.

00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:06.493
So I'm like yeah, yeah, you guys should
move, you guys should move here,

00:45:06.493 --> 00:45:08.070
move back to northern California.

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:12.900
So the combination of her wanting to
move to be closer to her parents and

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:16.080
him not really doing anything not having
a full time job also contributed.

00:45:18.200 --> 00:45:22.933
This guy Eugene who was one of our early
hires, he was working at a company,

00:45:22.933 --> 00:45:27.387
I actually knew him through my social
network, we are still friends.

00:45:27.387 --> 00:45:30.831
And he would always complained how
he hated his job and how there were

00:45:30.831 --> 00:45:34.840
trying to **** him over by promising
him stock options and never delivering.

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:39.507
He hated it, so I'm like, well,
here's a good opportunity.

00:45:39.507 --> 00:45:42.270
Let's see what else.

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:47.447
We hired this guy Michael who was in New
York, wasn't really doing anything also.

00:45:47.447 --> 00:45:51.533
So, he was a referral through
a friend of mine from Yahoo.

00:45:51.533 --> 00:45:54.637
So there was this guy Michael Radman
who I used to work with at Yahoo,

00:45:54.637 --> 00:45:57.264
who was working at a startup,
and we would keep in touch.

00:45:57.264 --> 00:46:01.104
And at some point I was complaining to him
how hard it is to find good engineers that

00:46:01.104 --> 00:46:01.724
are smart and

00:46:01.724 --> 00:46:05.017
capable and can get **** done and
don't just sit there and theorize.

00:46:05.017 --> 00:46:08.500
And he was like, I know a guy,
calls this guy Michael in New York.

00:46:08.500 --> 00:46:11.120
And so we randomly call this guy
Michael in New York, and I'm like, hey,

00:46:11.120 --> 00:46:13.160
Michael, I got your name
from other Michael.

00:46:13.160 --> 00:46:14.038
Do you want to come and interview?

00:46:14.038 --> 00:46:16.822
And I figured he would say no,
I'm pretty happy in New York.

00:46:16.822 --> 00:46:19.318
But he wasn't really doing much,
he was like, yeah, okay.

00:46:19.318 --> 00:46:20.910
So he came in and interviewed.

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:24.230
And so we had, let's see.

00:46:24.230 --> 00:46:26.700
We had one of the guys,
one of our engineers was in Russia,

00:46:26.700 --> 00:46:27.580
it was this guy Igor.

00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:30.491
He wasn't really doing
much in Russia also,

00:46:30.491 --> 00:46:32.189
it's not like they have
Silicon Valley in Russia.

00:46:32.189 --> 00:46:33.184
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:46:33.184 --> 00:46:37.781
we were like this band of outcasts
in some way, the group of people who

00:46:37.781 --> 00:46:42.159
weren't really doing much who got
together and built a product.

00:46:42.159 --> 00:46:46.251
But there were also people who were
working full time who we actually had to

00:46:46.251 --> 00:46:48.390
try really hard to convince to join.

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:51.755
One of the guys, Rick,
who helped us a lot with the backend

00:46:51.755 --> 00:46:55.068
as we were scaling the backend,
he was working at Yahoo.

00:46:55.068 --> 00:46:59.232
And so I think it took us six months
of meetings and dinners with me and

00:46:59.232 --> 00:47:01.141
Brian trying to convince him.

00:47:01.141 --> 00:47:03.850
And we would meet him, and
we would do it in a very subtle way.

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:05.130
Not like, come join us.

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:09.410
We were like, we have all these users and
we have all this gear.

00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:12.006
And we knew he was really, really
technical and he loved solving problems.

00:47:12.006 --> 00:47:16.722
So we weren't saying come join us and
we'll give you lots of money or

00:47:16.722 --> 00:47:18.307
options or whatever.

00:47:18.307 --> 00:47:20.820
We were playing a different angle
where we're saying, hi Rick,

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:21.880
if you're watching this.

00:47:21.880 --> 00:47:25.273
We were like, hey Rick, we're having all
these technical problems, and we did.

00:47:25.273 --> 00:47:29.353
And we're like, we just don't know,
there's this weird issue with FreeBSD 8

00:47:29.353 --> 00:47:32.110
where it computes for
kernel resources with Erlang.

00:47:32.110 --> 00:47:34.776
And Erlang is trying to
run on these 48 cores, and

00:47:34.776 --> 00:47:37.883
we don't know how there's some
contention in the kernel.

00:47:37.883 --> 00:47:40.570
If only we could figure it out,
we just need some help.

00:47:40.570 --> 00:47:43.850
And we knew that he loved doing
this kind of stuff, right?

00:47:43.850 --> 00:47:45.170
So with him, we played a different angle.

00:47:45.170 --> 00:47:46.560
It took us a few months to convince him,
but

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:49.820
eventually he joined and he helped us
fix a lot of bottlenecks in our system.

00:47:49.820 --> 00:47:55.260
So there were all different stories, but
I think the bulk of the initial kind of

00:47:55.260 --> 00:48:01.128
core of people who joined, they were from
our professional and personal networks.

00:48:01.128 --> 00:48:02.578
&gt;&gt; We are out of time, unfortunately.

00:48:02.578 --> 00:48:05.331
&gt;&gt; Man,
I can keep going as long as [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:05.331 --> 00:48:08.377
&gt;&gt; All right, if you got
&gt;&gt; All right,

00:48:08.377 --> 00:48:09.175
we'll go to one more question.

00:48:09.175 --> 00:48:10.559
Thank you.

00:48:10.559 --> 00:48:11.944
&gt;&gt; Sir.

00:48:11.944 --> 00:48:16.058
&gt;&gt; I think what's happening, sir,
role model will get off being focused.

00:48:16.058 --> 00:48:21.920
I want to understand your decision making
process when you get feedback from users.

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:25.498
How you define its said
noise to your product,

00:48:25.498 --> 00:48:29.830
how you define,
it's a key feature we need to improve.

00:48:29.830 --> 00:48:34.694
And how you find out that
the efficiency of communicating is

00:48:34.694 --> 00:48:38.980
a core feature you wanted focus on for
so many years.

00:48:38.980 --> 00:48:41.950
&gt;&gt; How do we know what features to
build and what features not to build?

00:48:41.950 --> 00:48:43.050
And you're absolutely right.

00:48:43.050 --> 00:48:46.587
Because in the early days, people would
write in and say, we want usernames so

00:48:46.587 --> 00:48:47.236
we want PINs.

00:48:47.236 --> 00:48:51.344
Because people were so conditioned by all
these messaging apps that came before us

00:48:51.344 --> 00:48:53.480
that you need to have a username or PIN.

00:48:53.480 --> 00:48:56.080
So if you were using BBM or
if you were using ICQ,

00:48:56.080 --> 00:48:59.860
you have some random PIN that you
would have to exchange with people.

00:48:59.860 --> 00:49:03.070
Or if you were using Skype or Yahoo
Messenger, you had to have a username.

00:49:03.070 --> 00:49:05.530
And people didn't understand
that what we were building was

00:49:05.530 --> 00:49:09.340
this whole new idea of like you
don't need any other stuff.

00:49:09.340 --> 00:49:11.058
You just sign up with your number,

00:49:11.058 --> 00:49:14.918
and it's connected to your phone that
has the same phone number, and you go.

00:49:14.918 --> 00:49:18.625
And so in early days, a lot of feedback
we took from users was useful.

00:49:18.625 --> 00:49:21.640
They're like, we want groups,
we want multimedia, we want

00:49:21.640 --> 00:49:25.390
to have additional privacy controls,
we want to turn off our last seen.

00:49:25.390 --> 00:49:27.700
Great, we built a lot
of what people asked.

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:30.830
But we also didn't build what people asked
because we didn't think it was the right

00:49:30.830 --> 00:49:31.990
fit for our product.

00:49:31.990 --> 00:49:36.680
Having the fundamental belief and the gut
feeling that what you're doing is right,

00:49:36.680 --> 00:49:40.840
and having that vision of,
it's just going to work.

00:49:40.840 --> 00:49:42.290
I'm going to build it using phone numbers.

00:49:42.290 --> 00:49:43.280
I'm not going to have usernames,

00:49:43.280 --> 00:49:46.712
I'm not going to have PINs because
it makes product more complicated.

00:49:46.712 --> 00:49:49.490
It makes product harder to use,
people forget their usernames and

00:49:49.490 --> 00:49:51.020
PINs and all that stuff.

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:53.370
Having that belief in yourself,

00:49:53.370 --> 00:49:58.710
and knowing that what you're building is
going to work is obviously also important.

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:01.913
So that's kind of how we
would make decisions.

00:50:05.380 --> 00:50:09.400
&gt;&gt; Okay, you go, and
then we'll take that for the 1 PM class.

00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:10.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah?
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:50:10.070 --> 00:50:14.790
What do you feel about security issue or
future security issue of Messenger app?

00:50:16.050 --> 00:50:18.710
&gt;&gt; What do I feel about security issue or
future security issue?

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:23.960
For us, well, as you know we
rolled out into an encryption.

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:26.510
And we weren't the first ones to do it,

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:30.110
obviously there were apps before
us that focused on security.

00:50:30.110 --> 00:50:32.900
But we were the first ones to
do it on such a global scale for

00:50:32.900 --> 00:50:34.539
everybody seamlessly, right?

00:50:34.539 --> 00:50:38.357
I mean there was no other app today that
has more than a billion people that has

00:50:38.357 --> 00:50:41.639
end to end encryption enabled by
default into everything you do,

00:50:41.639 --> 00:50:44.950
individual chats, group chats,
and everything else.

00:50:44.950 --> 00:50:47.150
So, we didn't start out.

00:50:47.150 --> 00:50:50.030
Again, this kind of goes back
to what we started with.

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:52.780
It was just a pure one-on-one messaging,
right?

00:50:52.780 --> 00:50:54.519
There was no group chat,
there was no multimedia,

00:50:54.519 --> 00:50:56.703
there was no enter an encryption,
there was no video callings,

00:50:56.703 --> 00:50:58.460
there was no voice phones,
there was none of that.

00:50:58.460 --> 00:51:01.835
But over years, we made a commitment to
our users that we were going to add all

00:51:01.835 --> 00:51:05.770
these features, and we were going to make
them work, and make them work really well.

00:51:05.770 --> 00:51:09.800
And so obviously, we feel strong is
that encryption is important and

00:51:09.800 --> 00:51:12.670
we feel strongly about Internet
encryption which is why we added it and

00:51:12.670 --> 00:51:14.930
which is why we have it
in our application today.

00:51:16.960 --> 00:51:17.801
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you so much.

00:51:17.801 --> 00:51:20.958
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

